    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Autism Spectrum Disorders (ASDs) are a broad spectrum of neurodevelopmental conditions with high prevalence but no effective therapy. Recently, mutations in the synaptic protein neuroligin-3 (Nlgn3) have been associated with ASDs, resulting in disrupted neuronal connectivity and altered neural circuit function. Although decreased white matter especially in the corpus callosum (CC) has been observed in patients with autism, the effect of autism-related mutations such as Nlgn3R451C on myelination by myelin-forming glial cells called oligodendrocytes has never been studied. Previous work has shown that this mutation leads to decreased Nlgn3 protein levels by 90% at the synapse, suggesting that it may disturb synapse formation between neurons and oligodendrocyte precursor cells (OPCs). In this work, we aim to determine how Nlgn3R451C alters myelination and OPC dynamics and to establish whether this mutation affects the adaptive proliferative response of OPCs to neuronal activity. Using immunostaining and fluorescence confocal microscopy in a conditional knock-in endogenous mouse model, this study reveals that Nlgn3R451C does not alter CC white matter volume. Furthermore, Nlgn3R451C is found to decrease the number of OPCs in white matter CC, but not in gray matter cortex, suggesting particular vulnerability for white matter OPCs. The OPC response to optogenetically increased neuronal activity shows unaltered proliferation despite potential disruption in neuron-to-OPC synapses in Nlgn3R451C. This study sheds light on the link between autism and myelin dynamics and establishes Nlgn3R451C-related myelin impairments that contribute to autism pathology.",
